%---------------------------------------------------------------------
\subsection{Early Warning Capability (Claim~2)}
\label{sec:earlywarning}

We analyze within-generation signal trajectories to determine how early collapse can be detected. At each evaluation checkpoint during generation~1 fine-tuning, we measure the percentage deviation of each signal from its 0\%-fraction baseline.

\paragraph{Signal separation at 3\% progress.} Table~\ref{tab:earlywarning} reports signal separation at step~50 (3\% of 1540 total steps). Stable rank achieves the largest separation: 36$\pm$4\% for 100\% contamination, consistent across the 0.5B baseline (34.4\%). Perplexity separation (20$\pm$3\%) and token entropy separation (26$\pm$3.5\%) are also detectable at 3\% progress, while spectral entropy (6.5$\pm$1.5\%) and gradient norm (3.5$\pm$1.2\%) require more training to separate from noise.

The 6-fraction resolution reveals a detection sensitivity limit: at 25\% contamination, stable rank separation is 6\% (above a 5\% detection threshold), but at 10\% contamination it is only 2.5\% (below threshold). The practical detection floor is therefore ${\sim}$20--25\% contamination at 3\% training progress.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{Early warning signal separation at step~50 (3\% progress, 1.5B, generation~1). Values are percentage deviation from 0\%-fraction baseline. Mean $\pm$ std across 3 seeds.}
\label{tab:earlywarning}
\small
\begin{tabular}{lccccc}
\toprule
Signal & 10\% vs 0\% & 25\% vs 0\% & 50\% vs 0\% & 75\% vs 0\% & 100\% vs 0\% \\
\midrule
Stable Rank   & $2.5\pm0.8$  & $6.0\pm1.5$  & $13\pm2.5$  & $22\pm3.5$   & $\mathbf{36\pm4}$ \\
Perplexity    & $1.2\pm0.5$  & $3.0\pm0.8$  & $4.0\pm1.0$ & $12\pm2.0$   & $20\pm3$ \\
Token Entropy & $1.8\pm0.6$  & $4.5\pm1.2$  & $10\pm2.0$  & $18\pm2.8$   & $26\pm3.5$ \\
Spectral Ent. & $0.5\pm0.3$  & $1.5\pm0.5$  & $2.5\pm0.8$ & $4.0\pm1.0$  & $6.5\pm1.5$ \\
Gradient Norm & $0.3\pm0.2$  & $0.8\pm0.4$  & $1.5\pm0.6$ & $2.0\pm0.8$  & $3.5\pm1.2$ \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\paragraph{Cross-generation acceleration.} Table~\ref{tab:acceleration} shows how collapse accelerates across generations. Under 100\% contamination, within-generation PPL change grows from +705\% (generation~1) to +2067\% (generation~2), an acceleration factor of 2.93$\times$. Stable rank saturates earlier: by generation~3, SR reaches 195 and plateaus, while PPL continues to grow. This divergent behavior between spectral and output signals motivates the signal taxonomy in Section~\ref{sec:taxonomy}.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{Cross-generation PPL acceleration (100\% contamination, 1.5B). Start/End PPL are evaluation values at step~0 and step~1540 respectively.}
\label{tab:acceleration}
\small
\begin{tabular}{lcccccc}
\toprule
Gen & PPL Start & PPL End & $\Delta$\% & SR End & Accel.\ vs prev \\
\midrule
0 & 13.5 & 13.8  & +2\%    & 26.5 & --- \\
1 & 20.5 & 165   & +705\%  & 140  & baseline \\
