15\% & 0.95 & 0.92 & 0.93 \\
20\% & 0.88 & 0.96 & 0.90 \\
25\% & 0.78 & 0.99 & 0.84 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}


%---------------------------------------------------------------------
\subsection{Signal Taxonomy and Operating Ranges (Claim~3)}
\label{sec:taxonomy}

Not all signals behave equally across severity levels. We analyze per-signal operating ranges using the 1.5B $\times$ 6-fraction $\times$ 3-seed data.

\paragraph{Operating range summary.} Table~\ref{tab:taxonomy} characterizes each signal's informative range. Stable rank is the best early indicator for the 0--75\% range but saturates beyond 75\%: the 75\%$\to$100\% stable rank increase is only 86\% compared to the 213\% jump at 50\%$\to$75\%. Perplexity does not saturate and tracks severity across the full range, making it the universal severity meter. Spectral entropy is uninformative below 25\% contamination.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{Signal operating range summary. $\bigstar$ = recommended signal for that severity range.}
\label{tab:taxonomy}
\small
\begin{tabular}{lcccccc}
\toprule
Signal & 0--10\% & 10--25\% & 25--50\% & 50--75\% & 75--100\% & Best Use \\
\midrule
Stable Rank   & Detect & $\bigstar$ High & $\bigstar$ High & $\bigstar$ High & Saturated & Early warn., 0--75\% \\
Spectral Ent. & Noise  & Low    & Medium & High    & Saturated & Moderate, 25--75\% \\
Perplexity    & Low    & Medium & $\bigstar$ High & $\bigstar$ High & $\bigstar$ High & Universal meter \\
Token Entropy & Noise  & Low    & Medium & $\bigstar$ High & $\bigstar$ High & Severe, 50--100\% \\
Gradient Norm & Noise  & Low    & Medium & Peak$\uparrow$ & $\downarrow$Drops & NOT recommended \\
HellaSwag     & Noise  & Noise  & Noise  & Weak    & Weak     & NOT recommended \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\paragraph{Phase transition at 75\%.} Table~\ref{tab:sensitivity} reveals the per-step signal sensitivity. The 50\%$\to$75\% transition produces the largest jumps across all signals: stable rank +213\%, PPL +1515\%, token entropy $-$42\%. This phase transition---from mild degradation to catastrophic collapse---is consistent across both domains and both model scales, suggesting it reflects a fundamental property of the contamination--collapse relationship.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{Per-step signal sensitivity (generation~4, 1.5B). $\Delta$\% = change from previous fraction level.}
\label{tab:sensitivity}
\small
\begin{tabular}{lccccc}
\toprule
Step & SR $\Delta$\% & PPL $\Delta$\% & TE $\Delta$\% & SE $\Delta$\% & GN $\Delta$\% \\
\midrule
0\%$\to$10\%   & +3    & +4      & $-$1   & +0.6   & +1 \\
10\%$\to$25\%  & +10   & +12     & $-$4   & +1.5   & +3 \\
25\%$\to$50\%  & +14   & +22     & $-$5   & +2.3   & +8 \\
50\%$\to$75\%  & \textbf{+213} & \textbf{+1515} & $-$42 & +12.5 & \textbf{+45} \\
75\%$\to$100\% & +86   & +210    & $-$50  & +9.0   & $-$20 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\paragraph{Gradient norm: a cautionary case.} Gradient norm peaks at 75\% contamination then \emph{decreases} by 20\% at 100\%. This non-monotonicity arises because at 75\%, the model faces conflicting gradients from real and synthetic data, increasing the norm. At 100\%, all data is synthetic; the model converges smoothly to a degenerate solution with a flat loss surface, reducing the gradient norm. A practitioner relying solely on gradient norm monitoring would receive a false ``all clear'' signal at the most extreme contamination---a safety-critical failure mode.

\paragraph{Downstream benchmarks fail.} HellaSwag accuracy shows only $-$5\% to $-$8\% degradation even at 100\% contamination on the 1.5B model. QLoRA fine-tuning primarily affects generation quality rather than the commonsense reasoning knowledge tested by HellaSwag. This demonstrates that \emph{passing benchmarks does not imply a healthy model}---weight-level spectral signals detect collapse that downstream metrics miss entirely.


%---------------------------------------------------------------------
\subsection{Per-Layer Collapse Propagation}
\label{sec:layerwise}

We perform layer-wise SVD analysis on the 1.5B model's 28 transformer layers to understand the spatial structure of collapse within the network.

\paragraph{Middle-out propagation.} Table~\ref{tab:layers} shows that core-middle layers (8--14) collapse first and most severely, with stable rank increasing 10$\times$ and singular value ratio $\sigma_1/\sigma_2 > 50$ (near rank-1). Boundary layers (embedding-adjacent and head-adjacent) are more resilient, showing only 3.8--4.1$\times$ stable rank increase. Collapse onset occurs at generation~1 for middle layers under 100\% contamination, but generation~2--3 for boundary layers (Table~\ref{tab:layeronset}).

