Lower-mid (4--7)   & gen 2 & gen 3 \\
Head-adjacent (22--27) & gen 2 & gen 3 \\
Embed-adjacent (0--3)  & gen 2 & gen 3--4 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

This middle-out pattern is physically grounded: middle layers encode the most abstract semantic features~\cite{tenney2019}, which are most sensitive to distributional shift. Boundary layers benefit from direct gradient pressure (head-adjacent) or low-level feature invariance (embedding-adjacent). Attention layers collapse 1.5$\times$ faster than FFN layers at the same depth, implicating the attention mechanism as the primary pathway for spectral degeneration---a finding relevant to designing targeted regularization strategies.


%---------------------------------------------------------------------
\subsection{Self-Recovery Phenomenon}
\label{sec:recovery}

An unexpected finding emerges at moderate contamination levels: \emph{partial self-recovery}. At 50\% contamination, perplexity peaks at generation~2 (20.5) then decreases to 19.2 by generation~4, a 6.3\% improvement. Token entropy shows a corresponding 8\% rebound. This pattern is reproduced across all 3 seeds. At 25\% contamination, the recovery is stronger, with near-baseline PPL by generation~4 (15.8 vs.\ 13.5 baseline). At 75\% contamination, no recovery is observed---degradation is continuous.

