\paragraph{Practical implication.} If a training corpus contains $\leq$50\% synthetic data, model collapse is self-limiting and monitoring suffices. Above ${\sim}$60\% contamination, active intervention---data filtering, mixing ratio adjustment---is necessary to prevent catastrophic collapse.


%---------------------------------------------------------------------
\subsection{Full Fine-Tuning Robustness Check}
\label{sec:fullft}

To verify that our findings are not artifacts of QLoRA's low-rank constraint, we compare QLoRA and full fine-tuning on Qwen2.5-0.5B (Table~\ref{tab:fullft}). Full fine-tuning produces \emph{more} severe collapse: PPL ratio increases from 53$\times$ (QLoRA) to 74$\times$ (full FT), and stable rank ratio from 6.6$\times$ to 8.0$\times$. The qualitative pattern is identical---monotonic severity tracking, same ranking across signals. QLoRA's rank-16 adapter constraint limits the degrees of freedom available for spectral degeneration; full fine-tuning, updating all ${\sim}$500M parameters, permits more complete collapse. Our QLoRA-based main results are therefore \emph{conservative estimates} of collapse severity under standard fine-tuning.

