\title{Training Dynamic Signals as Early Warning Systems for Model Collapse}

\author{
  Anonymous Authors
}

\begin{document}

\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
Model collapse---the progressive degradation of language models trained iteratively on synthetic data---poses a growing threat as LLM-generated text pervades training corpora. While the phenomenon is theoretically characterized, practitioners lack actionable tools to detect and quantify collapse severity \emph{during} training. We conduct the first systematic analysis of training dynamic signals as collapse severity monitors, evaluating four signal families (spectral, output, gradient, downstream) across six contamination levels, two model scales (0.5B, 1.5B), two text domains (WikiText-2, PubMed), and two fine-tuning methods (QLoRA, full fine-tuning). Our key findings are threefold. (1)~Spectral and output signals reliably track collapse severity with cross-fraction Spearman $\rho \geq 0.94$ ($n=6$, $p<0.005$), resolving prior statistical limitations of $n=3$ designs. (2)~Stable rank deviation detects collapse at 3\% training progress with 36\% signal separation, achieving AUC=0.97 as an early warning classifier. (3)~Different signal families have distinct operating ranges: stable rank saturates above 75\% contamination while perplexity tracks severity across the full range; gradient norm is non-monotonic and unsafe as a sole monitor. We additionally document a self-recovery phenomenon at $\leq$50\% contamination reproduced across 3/3 seeds, and show that larger models collapse more severely (71$\times$ vs.\ 53$\times$ perplexity ratio). Our signal taxonomy provides practitioners an actionable guide for choosing monitoring strategies at different severity levels.
\end{abstract}

%---------------------------------------------------------------------
\section{Introduction}
%---------------------------------------------------------------------

The proliferation of large language model (LLM)--generated text on the internet creates a feedback loop: models trained on web-scraped data increasingly consume their own outputs. Shumailov et al.~\cite{shumailov2024} showed that iterative training on model-generated data leads to \emph{model collapse}, a progressive degradation of output quality and diversity. Alemohammad et al.~\cite{alemohammad2024} formalized this as \emph{model autophagy disorder}, proving convergence to degenerate distributions under full synthetic replacement. Despite growing theoretical understanding, a practical gap remains: \textbf{how should practitioners detect and quantify model collapse during training?}

Current approaches to collapse detection are limited. Output-level methods~\cite{guo2024} require clean reference distributions; topological approaches~\cite{kalinowski2024} are computationally expensive; downstream benchmarks, as we show, fail to capture collapse until it is catastrophic. None provides a systematic comparison of \emph{which} training-time signals best track collapse across severity levels, nor do they examine within-generation temporal dynamics for early warning.

We address this gap through the first systematic study of training dynamic signals as model collapse monitors. Our experimental design covers:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Four signal families}: spectral (stable rank, spectral entropy), output (perplexity, token entropy), gradient (gradient norm), and downstream (HellaSwag accuracy).
    \item \textbf{Six contamination levels}: 0\%, 10\%, 25\%, 50\%, 75\%, 100\% synthetic data fractions, providing non-trivial statistical power ($n=6$ Spearman correlation, $p<0.005$).
    \item \textbf{Two model scales}: Qwen2.5-0.5B and Qwen2.5-1.5B, revealing that larger models are \emph{more} vulnerable to collapse.
    \item \textbf{Two text domains}: WikiText-2 (encyclopedic) and PubMed (biomedical), demonstrating domain-agnostic signal behavior.
    \item \textbf{Two fine-tuning methods}: QLoRA and full fine-tuning, confirming that collapse patterns are method-independent.
\end{itemize}

Our contributions are:

\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Signal--severity correlation} (Claim~1): Spectral and output signals track collapse severity with mean $|\rho| \geq 0.94$ across $n=6$ fractions ($p<0.005$), two scales, and two domains.
    \item \textbf{Early warning capability} (Claim~2): Stable rank deviation detects collapse at 3\% training progress with AUC=0.97, enabling a practical monitoring recipe (15\% threshold, 95\% sensitivity, 92\% specificity).
    \item \textbf{Signal taxonomy} (Claim~3): We provide the first characterization of per-signal operating ranges, identifying where each signal is informative, where it saturates, and where it misleads---giving practitioners an actionable guide.
\end{enumerate}

Additionally, we report a \emph{self-recovery phenomenon}: at $\leq$50\% contamination, perplexity peaks at generation~2 then decreases, reproduced across all seeds. This identifies a critical tipping point at ${\sim}$60--65\% contamination separating self-limiting degradation from catastrophic collapse.


%---------------------------------------------------------------------
\section{Related Work}
%---------------------------------------------------------------------

\paragraph{Model collapse.} Shumailov et al.~\cite{shumailov2024} demonstrated that iterative training on self-generated data causes progressive distribution narrowing. Alemohammad et al.~\cite{alemohammad2024} formalized the autophagy loop, proving convergence to degenerate fixed points. Dohmatob et al.~\cite{dohmatob2024} analyzed collapse dynamics in Gaussian mixture settings. These works characterize the phenomenon but do not address detection.

\paragraph{Collapse detection.} Guo et al.~\cite{guo2024} proposed SIGMA, monitoring output embedding spectra against a clean reference. Kalinowski~\cite{kalinowski2024} applied persistent homology to detect topological signatures of collapse. Both require either reference distributions or expensive computation. Our approach uses standard training-time signals that are free byproducts of the training loop.

\paragraph{Training dynamics monitoring.} Spectral analysis of neural network weights has been used for generalization prediction~\cite{martin2021}, pruning~\cite{tanaka2020}, and training stability~\cite{yang2023}. We extend this line of work to the specific setting of model collapse monitoring, where the signal taxonomy and operating ranges differ substantially from standard training.

\paragraph{Data contamination.} Detecting whether a model was trained on synthetic data is related to membership inference~\cite{shi2024} and watermarking~\cite{kirchenbauer2023}. Our work is complementary: rather than detecting \emph{whether} synthetic data is present, we monitor \emph{how severely} it degrades the model during training.


%---------------------------------------------------------------------
\section{Method}
%---------------------------------------------------------------------

\subsection{Iterative Collapse Protocol}

We simulate model collapse through iterative fine-tuning. Starting from a pretrained model $M_0$, each generation $g$ is produced by:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item Generating synthetic text from $M_{g-1}$ to form dataset $D^{\mathrm{syn}}_g$.
    \item Mixing with real data: $D_g = (1-\alpha) \cdot D^{\mathrm{real}} + \alpha \cdot D^{\mathrm{syn}}_g$, where $\alpha \in \{0, 0.1, 0.25, 0.5, 0.75, 1.0\}$.
    \item Fine-tuning $M_0$ on $D_g$ to obtain $M_g$.
\end{enumerate}
We run 5 generations ($g=0,\ldots,4$) per configuration. This protocol controls contamination level ($\alpha$) while maintaining the iterative feedback loop that drives collapse.

\subsection{Signal Families}

We monitor four families of training-time signals, computed periodically during fine-tuning:

\paragraph{Spectral signals.} For each weight matrix $W$ of the model (or a sampled subset of layers), we compute the singular value decomposition $W = U \Sigma V^\top$ and derive:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Stable rank}: $\mathrm{SR}(W) = \|W\|_F^2 / \|W\|_2^2 = \sum_i \sigma_i^2 / \sigma_1^2$. This measures effective dimensionality; collapse concentrates energy in fewer singular values, increasing stable rank.
    \item \textbf{Spectral entropy}: $H_\sigma = -\sum_i \hat{\sigma}_i \log \hat{\sigma}_i$ where $\hat{\sigma}_i = \sigma_i^2 / \sum_j \sigma_j^2$. This captures the uniformity of the singular value distribution.
\end{itemize}

\paragraph{Output signals.} Evaluated on a held-out validation set:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Perplexity} (PPL): Standard language modeling metric, $\mathrm{PPL} = \exp(-\frac{1}{N}\sum_{i}\log p(x_i | x_{<i}))$.
    \item \textbf{Token entropy}: Average entropy of the model's output distribution over the vocabulary, $H_{\mathrm{tok}} = -\frac{1}{N}\sum_i \sum_v p(v|x_{<i}) \log p(v|x_{<i})$.
\end{itemize}

\paragraph{Gradient signals.} The $\ell_2$ norm of the gradient vector computed on validation batches, reflecting training stability.

\paragraph{Downstream signals.} HellaSwag accuracy~\cite{zellers2019} as a proxy for commonsense reasoning capability.

\subsection{Experimental Configurations}

\paragraph{Core experiment (1.5B scale).} Qwen2.5-1.5B fine-tuned with QLoRA (rank 16) on WikiText-2, across 6 fractions $\times$ 3 seeds (42, 123, 7) $\times$ 5 generations = 90 runs. Training: 1540 steps, batch size 4, learning rate $2\times10^{-4}$.

\paragraph{Cross-domain validation.} Same 1.5B model on PubMed abstracts, 6 fractions $\times$ 1 seed $\times$ 5 generations = 30 runs.

\paragraph{Full fine-tuning comparison.} Qwen2.5-0.5B with full parameter updates on WikiText-2, 3 fractions $\times$ 1 seed $\times$ 5 generations = 15 runs.

\paragraph{Analysis experiments.} Within-generation early warning analysis, ROC threshold calibration, signal taxonomy, per-layer collapse propagation, and self-recovery analysis are derived from the above data without additional training (0 GPU-hours).

Total compute: ${\sim}$125 A6000 GPU-hours.


%---------------------------------------------------------------------
\section{Experiments and Results}
%---------------------------------------------------------------------

\subsection{Signal--Severity Correlation (Claim~1)}
\label{sec:claim1}

We evaluate whether training dynamic signals monotonically track contamination severity by computing Spearman rank correlation across 6 contamination fractions.

