\subsection{Early Warning Capability (Claim~2)}
\label{sec:earlywarning}

We analyze within-generation signal trajectories to determine how early collapse can be detected. At each evaluation checkpoint during generation~1 fine-tuning, we measure the percentage deviation of each signal from its 0\%-fraction baseline.

\paragraph{Signal separation at 3\% progress.} Table~\ref{tab:earlywarning} reports signal separation at step~50 (3\% of 1540 total steps). Stable rank achieves the largest separation: 36$\pm$4\% for 100\% contamination, consistent across the 0.5B baseline (34.4\%). Perplexity separation (20$\pm$3\%) and token entropy separation (26$\pm$3.5\%) are also detectable at 3\% progress, while spectral entropy (6.5$\pm$1.5\%) and gradient norm (3.5$\pm$1.2\%) require more training to separate from noise.

The 6-fraction resolution reveals a detection sensitivity limit: at 25\% contamination, stable rank separation is 6\% (above a 5\% detection threshold), but at 10\% contamination it is only 2.5\% (below threshold). The practical detection floor is therefore ${\sim}$20--25\% contamination at 3\% training progress.

