\paragraph{Gradient norm: a cautionary case.} Gradient norm peaks at 75\% contamination then \emph{decreases} by 20\% at 100\%. This non-monotonicity arises because at 75\%, the model faces conflicting gradients from real and synthetic data, increasing the norm. At 100\%, all data is synthetic; the model converges smoothly to a degenerate solution with a flat loss surface, reducing the gradient norm. A practitioner relying solely on gradient norm monitoring would receive a false ``all clear'' signal at the most extreme contamination---a safety-critical failure mode.

\paragraph{Downstream benchmarks fail.} HellaSwag accuracy shows only $-$5\% to $-$8\% degradation even at 100\% contamination on the 1.5B model. QLoRA fine-tuning primarily affects generation quality rather than the commonsense reasoning knowledge tested by HellaSwag. This demonstrates that \emph{passing benchmarks does not imply a healthy model}---weight-level spectral signals detect collapse that downstream metrics miss entirely.


