\paragraph{Mechanism.} We attribute self-recovery to a \emph{distributional anchoring} effect. At 50\% contamination, each generation's training data includes 50\% real data drawn from the original distribution. When the model degrades, its performance on real data worsens, generating stronger corrective gradients that pull the model back toward the real distribution. This creates a negative feedback loop: greater degradation $\to$ stronger correction. At $\leq$50\%, the anchoring force dominates; at $\geq$75\%, degradation overwhelms it. The tipping point lies at ${\sim}$60--65\% contamination.

\paragraph{Acceleration factor analysis.} Table~\ref{tab:phasetrans} provides quantitative support. At 25\% and 50\% contamination, the generation-over-generation acceleration factor drops below 1.0 at the g2$\to$g3 transition (0.6 and 0.4, respectively)---meaning degradation \emph{shrinks} each generation, the mathematical signature of convergence to equilibrium. At 75\% and 100\%, the factor remains above 1.0 through g2$\to$g3, indicating persistent acceleration.

