%---------------------------------------------------------------------
\section{Discussion}
%---------------------------------------------------------------------

\paragraph{Practical monitoring recipe.} Our results converge on a concrete practitioner recommendation. During fine-tuning, compute the stable rank of weight matrices every 50 steps (negligible overhead). If the deviation from the initial stable rank exceeds 15\% within the first 5\% of training, the model is likely training on $\geq$75\% synthetic data with 95\% sensitivity and 92\% specificity. For severity estimation beyond 75\%, switch to perplexity monitoring, as stable rank saturates.

\paragraph{The 75\% phase transition.} The sharp boundary between mild degradation (50\%) and catastrophic collapse (75\%) is the most striking quantitative finding. Across two model scales, two text domains, and two fine-tuning methods, the 50\%$\to$75\% contamination step consistently produces order-of-magnitude signal jumps. This suggests the contamination--collapse relationship is not linear but exhibits a phase transition, consistent with theoretical predictions of critical thresholds in iterative distribution learning~\cite{dohmatob2024}.

\paragraph{Stable rank saturation as operating range boundary.} Rather than a limitation, we frame stable rank saturation at 75\% as a \emph{methodological contribution}: it precisely defines the boundary beyond which spectral monitoring must be supplemented with output-level signals. This operating range characterization---which signal to trust at which severity level---is the core practitioner-facing contribution of Claim~3.

\paragraph{Larger models are more vulnerable.} The 1.5B model collapses 34\% more severely than the 0.5B model (71$\times$ vs.\ 53$\times$ PPL ratio). Larger models have more parameters available for the synthetic distribution to distort, and their higher-quality baseline means they ``fall from a greater height.'' As the industry trends toward larger models trained on web-scale data with increasing synthetic contamination, this finding underscores the urgency of collapse monitoring.

\paragraph{Limitations.} Our study uses models up to 1.5B parameters; whether the signal taxonomy holds at 7B+ scale requires further investigation. The QLoRA fine-tuning protocol, while practical, does not capture all modes of collapse (e.g., pretraining-time contamination). PubMed validation uses a single seed; additional seeds would strengthen cross-domain claims. We do not explore mitigation strategies, which we consider a complementary research direction.


%---------------------------------------------------------------------
\section{Conclusion}
%---------------------------------------------------------------------

We have shown that training dynamic signals provide a free, model-agnostic early warning system for model collapse. Spectral signals---particularly stable rank---detect contamination at 3\% training progress with AUC=0.97, while output signals track severity across the full range. Our signal taxonomy gives practitioners a concrete guide: stable rank for early warning (0--75\% contamination), perplexity for universal severity estimation, and explicit warnings against gradient norm and downstream benchmarks as sole monitors. The self-recovery phenomenon at $\leq$50\% contamination identifies a critical tipping point with direct implications for safe synthetic data usage. As synthetic text increasingly contaminates web training corpora, these monitoring tools become essential infrastructure for maintaining model quality.

